---
source: crates/wolf_mem/tests/mem_diagnostics.rs
expression: render_mem(src)
---
error[E1004]: this value is allocated in region `tmp`, but it is being stored into a value living in the caller's region
 --> mem:///main.lu:6:9
  |
4 |     var h = Holder { item: Item { value: 1, tag: "a" } }
  |                            --------------------------- the container is allocated here, into the caller's region
5 |     region tmp {
  |            --- region `tmp` is created here
6 |         h.item = Item { value: 2, tag: "b" }
  |         ^^^^^^ the two regions must be one
  |                  --------------------------- allocated here, into region `tmp`
  |
  = note: to keep the value, allocate it where it must live: build it outside the region block, or
    aim the allocation at a longer-lived region explicitly (`let r = region()` … `in r { …
    }`); widening the region block to cover every use also works. When the block's own value
    is what must outlive it, `copy region { … }` copies that value into the enclosing region
    before the free ([mem.region.copyout]). Two keep-alive alternatives change the ownership
    instead: `freeze` the region (immutable forever) or make the value a `shared` cell
    (reference-counted, never dangles).
  = note: this stores into a value declared outside the open `region` block. Such a store keeps
    nothing of the block when the stored value names no region (a scalar, a literal) or is
    written `copy e` at the store: that `copy` is a deep copy made in the outer value's
    region — the container's own for a `push`, an index or `Map` store or a `List`/`Map`
    replaced, the region ambient where the binding was declared for any other field
    [mem.region.outer-write].
